.Dd August 4, 2026
.Dt T150BOOT 8
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm t150boot
.Nd take a Thrustmaster wheel out of boot mode
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl nq
.Op Fl v Ar vid
.Op Fl p Ar pid
.Op Fl V Ar value
.Sh DESCRIPTION
Every T-series wheel enumerates at the shared boot product id
.Li 044f:b65d
and only reveals its own identity, and its own protocol, after a pair of
vendor control transfers on endpoint 0.
.Nm
issues them. Nothing else in this project works until it has.
.Pp
Sleep, wake and a replug all put the wheel back into boot mode, so this is
meant to run on every plug-in rather than once. It needs no privilege, so it
can be a user LaunchAgent that never asks for a password.
.Pp
It never claims a USB interface. An endpoint 0 device request does not need
one, claiming the interface macOS's HID driver already owns would be refused,
and
.Fn libusb_claim_interface
is currently reported to panic macOS 26.3 on Apple silicon.
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl n
Query the model and stop. Switches nothing.
.It Fl q
Say nothing unless something goes wrong, which is what a LaunchAgent wants.
.It Fl v Ar vid
Vendor id. The default is 0x044f.
.It Fl p Ar pid
The boot product id to look for. The default is 0xb65d.
.It Fl V Ar value
The switch value, which selects the model. The default is 0x0006, the T150
row of the kernel driver's table. Another wheel needs its own.
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
Exits 0 when the wheel was switched, and also when no wheel was found at the
boot id, because that is the ordinary state once one has been switched and a
tool that runs on every plug-in must not treat it as a failure. Exits 1 when
a wheel was found and could not be switched, and 2 on a usage error.
.Sh EXAMPLES
.Dl $ t150boot
.Dl attachment 0x06, model 0x03  T150
.Dl switched, the wheel is re-enumerating at 0xb677
.Pp
Check what a wheel is without touching it:
.Pp
.Dl $ t150boot -n
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr t150ctl 1 ,
.Xr t150d 8 ,
.Xr t150-probe 1
.Sh CAVEATS
The wheel leaves the bus the instant it accepts the switch, so the transfer
usually cannot complete. A stall, a not-responding or a no-device from it is
the normal outcome and is treated as success; only
.Xr t150-probe 1 Ns 's
.Nm probe_hid
can say whether the wheel really came back.
.Pp
This does not send the five initialisation packets the Linux driver puts on
the interrupt OUT pipe before the switch. Those need the device captured,
which needs root, and
.Nm probe_intr Fl I
is the tool for it. Whether they matter is unsettled: they were adopted here
to explain a wheel that came back apparently blocked, and that turned out to
be the autocenter holding at full strength rather than anything about the
switch. If a wheel misbehaves after this, try
.Nm t150ctl autocenter 0
before reaching for them.
.Sh AUTHORS
.An Renaud Allard
